      en: "Ouadane is an ancient settlement in the Adrar region of Mauritania, established in the 11th century. It thrived as a trans-Saharan trade hub for gold, salt, and slaves, connecting North Africa to Sub-Saharan regions. The town is renowned for its well-preserved mudbrick architecture and labyrinthine alleys, earning it UNESCO World Heritage status in 1996. Today, Ouadane has around 3,500 residents, primarily engaged in oasis agriculture and tourism. Its arid surroundings feature rocky plateaus and dry riverbeds. The site includes an old mosque and libraries holding centuries-old manuscripts. The nearby Richat Structure (Eye of the Sahara) is a geological wonder.",
